WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
Hi everyone, and welcome to Biology&nbsp;
Professor. Today, we're gonna go over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:09.210
how to conduct a Most Probable Number&nbsp;
test. So a Most Probable Number test,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:15.120
or MPN is sometimes how the method&nbsp;
is referred to as the NPN method,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:22.380
this is a method that is used to count&nbsp;
bacteria, to measure the amount of bacteria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:29.670
in a sample. And it is ideal for estimating&nbsp;
the number of microbes in very dilute samples.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:39.150
So when we say dilute, very dilute samples,&nbsp;
we mean samples where the bacteria are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:45.150
very concentrated. So we're not talking about lab&nbsp;
plates or lab cultures where you can do a plate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:51.900
count or an optical density. These are samples&nbsp;
where there are much fewer bacteria present or at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:57.870
least that's what you suspect. An example of this&nbsp;
would be various kinds of water and food testing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:03.870
so for example, testing certain kinds&nbsp;
of water sources for coliform bacteria.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:12.990
Coliform bacteria are bacteria that can ferment&nbsp;
lactose and we have a lot of coliform bacteria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:19.860
in human feces and so if there's coliforms in&nbsp;
water, that's typically indicative of fecal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:26.940
contamination. So the way the MPN method works&nbsp;
is it uses changes in either color of broth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:32.820
or changes in turbidity, that's the cloudiness&nbsp;
that you see when bacteria are growing in liquid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:38.910
culture, so it looks for either color change&nbsp;
or turbidity change to detect bacterial growth.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:53.100
And then you look at how many tubes either changed&nbsp;
color or have noted turbidity and you can count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:58.710
those tubes and then interpret your results by&nbsp;
comparing your results to a standard reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:03.000
table. And we'll go over an example of that in&nbsp;
a second, but when you are using the standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.790
reference table, it's important that you realize&nbsp;
that the reference table will give you a number,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:18.720
a most probable number an MPN, and then an MPN is&nbsp;
equal to 1 CFU. CFU is a common way of referring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:28.800
to bacteria and it stands for "colony forming&nbsp;
unit". And basically that just means one viable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:39.720
cell. So, just to recap, this method gives you&nbsp;
a number, a most probable number, for how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:45.960
bacteria are in a certain sample and that equates&nbsp;
to one colony forming unit, which is the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:51.720
thing as saying one viable cell, so one living&nbsp;
cell, one cell that's capable of reproducing. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.720 --> 00:03:02.580
let's go through an example. First, you have your&nbsp;
inoculation. And so in this example, we're looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:08.010
at a water sample. Perhaps it's from a pond,&nbsp;
perhaps it's from a recreational swimming area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:14.340
perhaps it's a drinking water sample - we have&nbsp;
some water sample here and then we have three sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:21.180
of five lactose broth tubes. And remember that in&nbsp;
this example, we're looking for coliform bacteria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:29.190
and coliform bacteria can ferment lactose. You&nbsp;
could do this method with other kinds of tests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:34.260
where you're looking for a different kind of&nbsp;
color change or a different growth in turbidity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:39.690
and different kinds of broth, depending on the&nbsp;
type bacteria you're looking at. So here we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:46.620
our three sets of five lactose brought tubes and&nbsp;
what we do is we take ten milliliters from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:53.160
drinking water sample and inoculate each of these&nbsp;
five tubes - so ten milliliters added to each one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:59.850
then we take one milliliter of the water sample&nbsp;
and add it to each of these tubes, and then we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:08.430
take 0.1 milliliters of water from this sample and&nbsp;
add it to each of these tubes. And so we're having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:15.060
this tenfold decrease each time in the amount&nbsp;
of water that's being added and so theoretically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:20.850
in the amount of viable coliforms that are being&nbsp;
added to these tubes. Then we incubate overnight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:26.340
at 37 degrees. Typically, you want to incubate&nbsp;
for like 24 hours for this particular method,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:31.170
then it's time to read results and what that&nbsp;
means is you're looking for the color change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:38.340
So when coliform bacteria are present in these&nbsp;
broth tubes, they will ferment the lactose and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:45.660
that results in a color change. And so, for this&nbsp;
particular test, we have four positive tubes here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:53.130
So four of them showed a color change, one did&nbsp;
not. We have three positive tubes here, three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:58.950
showed a color change, two did not. And then where&nbsp;
we were adding the smallest amount of the water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:07.680
sample, we've just got one positive tube, so one&nbsp;
changed color and four did not. And so that is our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:14.040
results that we then want to come and compare in&nbsp;
the reference table. So our pattern, if you will,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:23.280
was 4-3-1 and so we look at our reference table.&nbsp;
If you're a student, this is often in the back of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:32.640
a textbook or in the back of like a lab manual. So&nbsp;
you look for the pattern of 4-3-1 and this tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:41.630
you that if you see the 4-3-1 pattern, then the&nbsp;
MPN, the most probable number, of coliforms in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:49.940
original sample was 33 per 100 mL. Now, if we had&nbsp;
gotten different patterns up here, if it had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:58.400
4-4-1 or if it had been 5-2-1 - different patterns&nbsp;
will result in a different most probable number,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:07.340
but with this pattern of 4-3-1, the best answer&nbsp;
statistically is that for every 100 milliliters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:16.760
of that sample we have 33 coliform bacteria.&nbsp;
The table also gives us a 95% confidence range,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:26.330
and for the pattern 4-3-1, the 95% confidence&nbsp;
range is 11 to 93, so the most probable number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:32.810
is 11 to 90 per 100 milliliters and all this&nbsp;
means is that whereas 33 is like the best guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:38.600
the most sort of statistically supported&nbsp;
guess, you can be 95% confident that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:47.030
in this water sample that gave this pattern,&nbsp;
there is somewhere between 11 and 93 viable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:54.890
coliform bacteria per 100 mL of this sample. So&nbsp;
that is how you conduct an MPN test. If you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:59.450
to learn more about bacterial growth, you can&nbsp;
check out my video on bacterial growth curves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:03.530
and binary fission, but that's it for today.&nbsp;
Thanks for watching Biology Professor! :)

